WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.900
Speaker 1: Today. We welcome to the program.&nbsp;
Matthew Connolly, who's a Columbia history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:10.260
professor and also author of the book The&nbsp;
Declassification Engine What History Reveals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:14.329
About America's Top Secrets. A really great&nbsp;
having you on today. I appreciate your time.&nbsp;

00:00:14.329 --> 00:00:17.400
Speaker 2: Great to be with you, David.
Speaker 1: So, you know, we've been talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:23.700
about these recent classified documents. I guess&nbsp;
it's fine to call them scandals or news stories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:29.520
involving Donald Trump and several hundred&nbsp;
documents. And then Joe Biden and Mike Pence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:35.580
reportedly with a small number of documents that&nbsp;
they didn't appear to be deliberately keeping at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:39.300
their homes or offices, which is certainly a&nbsp;
difference to what we know so far about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:43.740
Trump story. But there's sort of like a zooming&nbsp;
out that we can do more generally to think about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:50.340
what sort of documents are classified. Are we&nbsp;
classifying the right number of documents? Are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:55.680
there things that are over classified and actually&nbsp;
reducing transparency? So maybe to start with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:01.620
what is the history of this concept of these sorts&nbsp;
of classifications that that have been created?&nbsp;

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:07.200
Speaker 2: Yeah, well, you might think that&nbsp;
this is something that governments do. You know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:12.660
you could think of it like death and taxes, right?&nbsp;
That government officials would like to operate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:18.420
under the cloak of secrecy. But the fact is, the&nbsp;
United States, compared to other countries anyway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:25.500
was really an outlier. You know, for the first 150&nbsp;
years, the U.S. was quite remarkable. You know, I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:31.500
think a lot of people probably know we'd never had&nbsp;
a Central Intelligence Agency. We we also, unlike&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:35.580
other countries, we didn't have what they used to&nbsp;
call black chambers, where they would intercept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:42.420
and decode communications. I mean, almost every&nbsp;
other country the size of the U.S. had the means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:48.240
and actually the motivation to track subversives.&nbsp;
They would intercept their mail and so on. In the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:53.460
United States, that was a felony. So, you know,&nbsp;
both on the one hand, it was a matter of the U.S.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:58.620
government not really creating a lot of secrets.&nbsp;
And then on the other hand, people had a very high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:03.540
expectation as to their own personal privacy.&nbsp;
So what we now take for granted, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:08.640
what some people call the national secrecy&nbsp;
complex, You know what I call the dark state. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:12.900
really an invention of the last 80 years or so.&nbsp;
It really only started after the Second World War.&nbsp;

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:18.660
Speaker 1: And when we talk about what types&nbsp;
of things are classified, secret, top secret,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:25.440
etc., all these different designations, I think&nbsp;
the assumption is always that these are extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:32.940
sensitive pieces of information, often related&nbsp;
to probably more foreign policy than domestic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:37.500
policy. I think that's the assumption. But&nbsp;
there's relatively mundane things that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:40.800
also designated in these ways.
Speaker 2: Oh, sure. You know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:47.520
so it's interesting. There's a kind of, you&nbsp;
know, dual dialog going on about secrecy where,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:51.660
you know, when you bring out people who have&nbsp;
their security clearances and spent years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:55.620
in the intelligence community or what have&nbsp;
you. Yeah. They'll tell you about all these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.620 --> 00:03:01.620
rules and how elaborate they are and how careful&nbsp;
people have to be. But the reality is, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:07.200
if you actually study this history and you look at&nbsp;
what people do every day, they'll tell you, yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:13.260
you know, I had to meet a colleague for coffee&nbsp;
and I classified that email as top secret because,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:18.360
you know, at some point in that long email chain,&nbsp;
by the time we finally figure out, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:23.400
which Starbucks you're going to I may have said&nbsp;
something about some program, you know, that could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:29.400
be classified. So better safe than sorry. You&nbsp;
know, and this is how you end up with what is now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:35.520
a completely unmanageable situation. You know, the&nbsp;
State Department generates some 2 billion email a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:42.360
year. So just think of the volume of these coffee,&nbsp;
you know, emails that end up being top secret,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:48.480
where for decades nobody's ever going to see them&nbsp;
unless somebody reviews that stuff page by page to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:52.620
decide the rest of us are allowed to read it.
Speaker 1: So it seems that the sort of most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:58.140
obvious concern one would have about&nbsp;
overclassification would be it reduces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:02.220
transparency. There are documents here that,&nbsp;
you know, maybe we don't post everything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:06.900
but at least through FOIA requests should&nbsp;
be available if someone wanted them. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:11.280
you reduce transparency if you over&nbsp;
classify. Is it that simple or what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:16.980
other concerns are there with overclassification?
Speaker 2: Well, you know, for me as a historian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:22.500
I care a lot, you know, about. But a lot of other&nbsp;
people, too, would say is the court of history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:27.180
you know, because, you know, if and it's true,&nbsp;
we have 18 different intelligence agencies. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:33.360
have an $800 billion Defense Department. We have&nbsp;
this vast apparatus that's carrying out covert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:38.460
operations, you know, targeted assassinations.&nbsp;
You know, wouldn't you like to think that at some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:43.800
point, you know, someone is going to be able to&nbsp;
look at all the evidence? Right. To even if it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:48.120
only the court of history to hold our leaders to&nbsp;
account? You know, but I could tell you that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:53.820
just not going to happen, you know, because even&nbsp;
before the era of electronic records, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:59.700
this age of big data, the National Archives&nbsp;
could only keep about 1 to 3% of the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:05.340
documentary record. And if it's the case and it's&nbsp;
true, we now have just to take another example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:11.520
we have one intelligence agency that's producing&nbsp;
a petabyte of classified data every single year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:17.280
If those were paper records and they were lined&nbsp;
up in file cabinets and file folders, it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:22.380
circle the equator. And even the government&nbsp;
says it would take 2 million archivists just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:27.240
to go through that to decide what the rest of us&nbsp;
are allowed to know. So yeah, that's important. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:31.440
think it matters because if you think, you know,&nbsp;
history matters and ultimately, you know, holding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:35.280
our leaders to account, that matters quite a lot.&nbsp;
But I'll tell you the other reason it matters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:41.040
and this is also not trivial when they can't&nbsp;
decide what's actually secret, they can't protect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:45.720
the stuff that really could kill people. And we've&nbsp;
just seen one after another, you know, and these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:51.840
really are scandals like NSA, CIA hacking tools,&nbsp;
you know, getting stolen. We've seen, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:55.920
the Chinese government apparently taking tens&nbsp;
of millions of personnel records with which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.920 --> 00:06:01.080
they could use to blackmail people who do have top&nbsp;
secret security clearances. Right. So that, too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:07.380
is a real danger and it's a danger here and now.
